2. Request-Driven Response Codes — Technical Detail

How it works in FT today:
Each service returns a numeric status code as part of its response, and the calling channel branches its own behavior based on that code (e.g. showing a specific error message, retrying, or proceeding to the next step). These codes are business-rule outcomes, not generic HTTP-style errors — each one maps to a specific condition the service detected during processing.

Below is the current code inventory by service, since you asked for the specific detail:

Create Outward Transfer: 0000 success; 0006 generic failure (insufficient balance / OFS rejection / channel restriction); 0011 insufficient balance (Business Banking specific case); 3003 posting restricted (customer industry restriction).

Simulate Fees & Calculate Commission: 0000 success; 0006 failure; 1001 duplicate/cached replay; 5000 external reference, channel, or inputter mismatch against an in-flight record.

Amend Outward Transfer: 0000 success; 0006 failure (insufficient balance or OFS rejection).

Authorize FT: 0000 success (CUSTODY channel only); 1 success (all other channels — note this is a single-digit code, not the 4-digit format used elsewhere); -1 generic failure (non-CUSTODY); 0008 failure default (CUSTODY); 0020 inputter/ownership mismatch guard.

Create Nostro Transfer: 0000 success; 0006 failure; 1001 duplicate/cached replay.

FT Details (enquiry): 0000 found; 0001 not found.

DXP (digital channel) Execute Payment: 0000 success; 0001 debit account not found; 0005 credit account not found; 0011 insufficient balance; 0020 auto-authorization/record-status issue; 0021 ambiguous OFS outcome; 0023 domestic-currency country-control rejection; 0024 IBAN-related outcome; 0035 KYC needs updating; 0099 staff self-transfer blocked; 1001 duplicate; 1111 generic/unresolved error; 2222 / 3333 FX-not-supported rejections; 4444 invalid charity account; 9999 retry limit exhausted.

Important nuance we need TPH to account for: some numeric codes are reused across different services with DIFFERENT meanings (for example, 0020 means something different in Authorize FT than it does in DXP), and the code FORMAT itself isn't even consistent (Authorize FT returns a bare 1 / -1 for most channels instead of a 4-digit code). We need to decide, as a requirement, whether TPH should:
(a) preserve each service's existing codes exactly as-is (so no channel-side changes are needed), or
(b) move to one clean, non-overlapping status code model shared across all services (cleaner long-term, but means every calling channel needs to be updated to the new codes).

